Fillets and chamfers: a contact along a closed or circular edge of its
face, the face exactly as wide as the blend (mitcad#240).

The cases: the inner rim of a step ring (a hole above a counterbore)
chamfered by the ring's width, the rim a whole circle or two arcs; the
top of a lug inside a bore, a ring sector as wide as the chamfer of its
edge with the bore's wall. 0014 splits an analytic blend whose contact
line lies on its face's boundary by the domains of the same line moved a
little into the face, and stores a blend whose contact covers a whole edge
of its face as a blend on that edge (the face is dropped). That handled
lines and open edges only:

- A circular contact (on a plane, a blend along a circular edge) is moved
  into the face by changing its radius; translating it crossed the
  boundary (HatchOnBoundary). A whole circle is hatched from a point
  outside the face, so that no domain runs across its start (the
  hatcher's domain there had no first point), and a domain across the
  start of a closed line is sampled across it. A closed line inside the
  face all round, on the boundary at every sample, is one domain.
- A line on the boundary that the hatcher did split, but into partial
  domains (inside along some of it, on the boundary along the rest), is
  hatched on the boundary as well (LineOnBoundary: on the boundary at two
  of 15 samples or more); a line partly along an edge keeps the hatcher's
  domains, as the line moved inside has domains off the boundary.
- On a closed spine, a piece reaching once round from before the start
  of the spine's first edge (the contact on a cylinder whose seam is not
  at the spine's vertex) had both ends at the same point of the spine and
  was dropped; its start is now taken a period earlier (SplitKPart), as
  for a piece whose ends came in reverse order.
- A closed contact along a closed edge (or the pieces of one) counts as
  along the edge (runsAlongEdge accepted open edges only). Its ends at the
  edge's vertex are that vertex (in the stripe's points too), the
  contact's curve is cut at the vertex where the vertex lies inside it,
  and where a closed spine starts elsewhere on the edge, the edge is cut
  there with none of it kept (ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges).

Before, the chamfers of the cases failed (the blend was walked along the
boundary: "Path failed", or not implemented for chamfers) or gave an
invalid shape (the face beyond the edge reduced to the contact's edge).
Still open: a whole closed contact along a closed edge whose stripe
starts away from the edge's vertex and away from the seam of the face
beyond gave an unorientable face; 0144 builds it.

